AB  - Blockchain has built a decentralized trust system via distributed ledgers and cryptography, but faces severe security threats from quantum computing and complex attacks; this paper proposes an innovative hierarchical defense model(HDM) that constructs a security barrier from three dimensions: the encryption layer adopts NIST postquantum standard Dilithium-III(NIST Level-3, approximately equivalent to AES-192) to replace traditional elliptic curve encryption, using lattice-based signatures to resist Shor attacks and optimized polynomial ring parameters to achieve 1.5 ms signature generation (30 % faster than ECDSA); the consensus layer designs a reputation-driven dynamic DPoS mechanism(with real-time reputation updates) combined with sharding, reducing network communication overhead by 40% and avoiding static sharding defects of existing schemes; the contract layer proposes a Coq-based automated formal verification framework(supporting non-functional attribute verification) to convert Solidity code into high-order logic, filling the manual reliance gap of existing theorem provers. Experimental results show HDM's Dilithium-III resists Shor, Grover, and lattice-based quantum attacks(key cracking cost 106 times that of RSA-2048), with 98% survival rate against 51% attacks and 95% smart contract vulnerability detection rate(false positive rate (<)5 %); the paper also designs a crossdomain data sharing protocol(IPFS + PRE + ZKP) integrating post-quantum cryptography to support privacyprotected collaboration for industrial IoT devices, providing theoretical and technical support for blockchain in highsecurity scenarios such as financial transactions and military communications. Β© 2025 IEEE.
