AB  - With the rapid advancement and broad implementation of Vehicular Ad hoc Networks (VANET), ensuring system security and safeguarding user privacy have emerged as a core topic of concern for both academia and industry. Secure vehicle-to-roadside unit (V2R) communication fundamentally relies on robust authentication and key agreement (AKA) schemes. While a large number of AKA schemes have been introduced, existing approaches face significant limitations: vulnerability to emerging quantum computing threats, excessive certificate management overhead, and impractical communication or computation costs. The current quantum-resistant AKA schemes can secure communication to a certain extent, but they have issues with heavy certificate management cost and substantial communication and computation cost. To address the mentioned challenges, we propose a novel quantum-resistant blockchain-assisted certificateless authentication and three-party key agreement (BCL-3AKA), which effectively solves the problems of key escrow and high communication and computation cost. Smart contracts are utilized to achieve automated key verification and management, while ensuring the transparency and tamper-proofness of the key generation process. Formal and informal security proofs confirm that BCL-3AKA is effective in protecting data security and ensuring secure data transmission between RSUs and vehicles. Moreover, experimental results indicate that this scheme significantly decreases the communication and computation overhead. Thus, BCL-3AKA provides an efficient and reliable solution for secure communication in the VANET environment. Β© 2025 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
