AB  - The evolution of traditional power systems into smart grids has introduced unprecedented opportunities for efficiency, reliability, and sustainability, while simultaneously expanding the cybersecurity attack surface. As a cyber-physical system, the smart grid is vulnerable to threats ranging from advanced persistent threats (APTs) and supply chain attacks to insider risks and AI-driven exploits. Real-world incidents, such as the Ukraine power grid attack and SolarWinds breach, underscore the urgent need for robust defenses. This chapter explores the threat landscape, standards and regulatory frameworks (e.g., NERC CIP, NIST, International Electrotechnical Commission, IEEE), and architectural approaches such as defense-in-depth and zero trust. Emerging technologies, including post-quantum cryptography, blockchain, and intrusion detection systems, are examined for their role in enhancing resilience and privacy. With global perspectives from regions such as Asia, Africa, and the EU, the chapter emphasizes that securing the smart grid requires multilayered strategies, cross-sector collaboration, and continuous innovation to ensure a reliable and secure energy future. Β© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
