AB  - Two-factor authenticated key exchange (2fAKE) protocol requires a client to provide two authentication factorsΒ to authenticate itself and establish a shared session key withΒ a server, thus providing double-insurance. Β Β Β Β In this work, we focus on 2fAKE with biometricsΒ and passwords serving as the two factors. Such 2fAKE might beΒ the most convenient one for the client. However, the use of biometrics and passwords may increase the risk of their leakage. To protectΒ the privacy of biometrics and passwords, we formalize an enhanced security model for 2fAKE. Specifically, we require zero-knowledge on the biometrics, namely the server databaseΒ as well as the protocol transcripts do not reveal any informationΒ about the biometrics. As for passwords, we require that the compromiseΒ of the server database does not leak the passwords of clients. Β Β Β Β We then propose a generic construction of 2fAKE,Β which enjoys both great convenience and enhanced security. It doesΒ not require the client to store any secrets, does not storeΒ the passwords in plain in the server database, and achieves zero-knowledge on the biometrics throughout the protocol.Β By instantiating the generic construction with post-quantum secure building blocks, we immediately get concrete 2fAKE protocolΒ with enhanced security from post-quantum assumptions,Β which provides even stronger security guarantees. The experimentsΒ show that our 2fAKE instantiation achieves high efficiency, whereΒ the server stores just 3.1 KB in database for one client,Β the communication is only 3 rounds and costs 4.3 KB, the client runsΒ in 25 ms and the server runs in only 0.2 ms. Β© The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
