AD  - Princess Nourah bint Abdulrahman University, College of Computer and Information Sciences, Department of Information Technology, P.O. Box 84428, Riyadh, 11671, Saudi Arabia
AB  - The adoption of 6G networks introduces unprecedented security challenges that extend beyond the capabilities of current authentication protocols. EP-SeaAKA extends SeaAKA authentication for 6G networks by addressing critical vulnerabilities while maintaining backward compatibility. The protocol introduces three key innovations: (1) privacy-preservation using zero-knowledge proofs and homomorphic encryption, protecting identities during re-authentication while enabling service provisioning; (2) quantum-resistant primitives based on R-LWE problem with formal security reduction; (3) RIS-aware authentication leveraging synchronized reflection patterns as spatiotemporal factors. Security analysis provides R-LWE hardness reduction with parameters (n = 2048, q = 12289, Οƒ = 3.2) and differential privacy guarantees (Οµ = 0.1, Ξ΄ < 10β’6). Performance evaluation shows EP-SeaAKA adds only 8.7% computational overhead during initialization while achieving 36% re-authentication latency reduction and maintaining strong scalability. Β© 1997-2012 IEEE.
