AB  - In the realm of post-quantum cryptography,Β the Commutative Supersingular Isogeny Diffie-Hellman (CSIDH) hasΒ drawn considerable attention since its proposal at Asiacrypt 2018.Β This paper introduces a new batching strategy for computing multiple group actions in CSIDH, which is essential for constructing cryptographic schemes such as zero-knowledge proofs and signature schemes. Two variants of the batching strategy are presented inΒ this work, suited to different security requirements and application contexts. In the first scenario, we focus on situations whereΒ group actions are public, aiming to reduce CSIDHβ€™s computational cost. Using our strategy, we show that computational costs canΒ be significantly reduced, making it a viable option for schemesΒ in which efficiency is critical. This variant reduces the computational requirements of group actions by roughly up to 14% when compared to non-batched action evaluation. The second variant is towards constant-time group actions, and it reduces computationalΒ costs while maintaining resilience to side-channel timing attacks.Β This article also introduces a new constant-time implementation ofΒ CSIDH that, when combined with the second variant, reduces the computation costs of secret action sets by roughly up to 8% comparedΒ to individual computation using state-of-the-art constant-time algorithms, while the new constant time alone reduces computationΒ by approximately up to 4%. Β© The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2025.
