AB  - The cybersecurity landscape of the energy sector has evolved rapidly, driven by the integration of digital technologies, decentralized generation, and interconnected operational technologies. This connectivity expands the attack surface, making infrastructure vulnerable to cyber threats. This chapter explores the evolving threat landscape, identifying key risks such as SCADA vulnerabilities, supply chain attacks, ransomware, and AI-driven adversarial techniques, including AI misuse for automated vulnerability discovery or attacks. It also examines regulatory frameworks like NERC CIP, IEC 62443, and DOE guidelines, which set security standards to ensure resilience. Beyond identifying risks, this chapter discusses comprehensive cybersecurity frameworks and mitigation strategies. It maps proactive defense strategies to established methodologies such as the NIST Cybersecurity Framework, ISO 27001, and Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA), evaluating AI-powered detection, segmentation, blockchain, and automated responses. These approaches help energy operators enhance security posture, mitigate risks, and reduce disruptions. The chapter presents a detailed analysis of major cyber incidents targeting energy infrastructure, including the Stuxnet worm, Ukraine power grid attacks, and the Colonial Pipeline ransomware. These cases reveal adversarial tactics, vulnerabilities, and security gaps. The final section explores future directions and research challenges in energy cybersecurity, covering AI threat intelligence, post-quantum cryptography, resilience, and global cooperation. It emphasizes the need for interdisciplinary collaboration and proactive policy to safeguard future energy systems. Blending technical depth and case studies, this chapter is a key resource for researchers, policymakers, and professionals strengthening energy cybersecurity. Β© 2025
