AB  - This study looks at how artificial intelligence(AI) is affecting cybersecurity in the banking and financial industries, with a particular emphasis on creative ways to improve security protocols. AI makes it possible to continuously evaluate the reliability of users and transactions, thwarting sophisticated threats like fraud and insider attacks. To guarantee that AI-driven security decisions are visible and understandable, which is a prerequisite for adhering to regulatory standards, the chapter highlights the necessity of explainable AI. It addresses important privacy problems by examining AI-powered zero-knowledge proofs to secure transactions and protect sensitive data. In addition, issues like regulatory complexity and AI model driftβ€”the degradation of systems as a result of changing conditionsβ€”are covered. The chapter suggests ethical hacking frameworks based on AI to find vulnerabilities before they happen. Looking ahead, it stresses the potential of AI in cyber resilience planning to simulate attack scenarios and boost recovery tactics, along with the creation of quantum-resistant AI security models to prepare for growing dangers posed by quantum computing. The study presents AI as a crucial instrument for improving financial network security in an increasingly complicated digital environment through these findings. Β© 2026 selection and editorial matter, Alex Khang individual chapters, the contributors.
