AB  - In this paper, we show how to use the Matrix Code Equivalence (MCE) problem as a new basis to construct signature schemes. This extends previous work on using isomorphism problems for signature schemes, a trend that has recently emerged in post-quantum cryptography. Our new formulation leverages a more general problem and allows for smaller data sizes, achieving competitive performance and great flexibility. Using MCE, we construct a zero-knowledge protocol which we turn into a signature scheme named Matrix Equivalence Digital Signature (MEDS). We provide an initial choice of parameters for MEDS, tailored to NISTβ€™s Category 1 security level, yielding public keys as small as 2.8Β kB and signatures ranging from 18Β kB to just around 6.5Β kB, along with a reference implementation in C. Β© 2023, The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.
