AB  - From the minimal assumption of post-quantum semi-honest oblivious transfers, we build the first Ξµ -simulatable two-party computation (2PC) against quantum polynomial-time (QPT) adversaries that is both constant-round and black-box (for both the construction and security reduction). A recent work by Chia, Chung, Liu, and Yamakawa (FOCSβ€™21) shows that post-quantum 2PC with standard simulation-based security is impossible in constant rounds, unless either NPβ† BQP or relying on non-black-box simulation. The Ξµ -simulatability we target is a relaxation of the standard simulation-based security that allows for an arbitrarily small noticeable simulation error Ξµ. Moreover, when quantum communication is allowed, we can further weaken the assumption to post-quantum secure one-way functions (PQ-OWFs), while maintaining the constant-round and black-box property. Our techniques also yield the following set of constant-round and black-box two-party protocols secure against QPT adversaries, only assuming black-box access to PQ-OWFs: extractable commitments for which the extractor is also an Ξµ -simulator;Ξµ -zero-knowledge commit-and-prove whose commit stage is extractable with Ξµ -simulation;Ξµ -simulatable coin-flipping;Ξµ -zero-knowledge arguments of knowledge for NP for which the knowledge extractor is also an Ξµ -simulator;Ξµ -zero-knowledge arguments for QMA. At the heart of the above results is a black-box extraction lemma showing how to efficiently extract secrets from QPT adversaries while disturbing their quantum states in a controllable manner, i.e., achieving Ξµ -simulatability of the after-extraction state of the adversary. Β© 2022, International Association for Cryptologic Research.
